fozia Manita was paralyzed below the  waist when she was only two years old  like many disabled people in Libya a  lack of infrastructure means it's  difficult for her to move around  growing up I couldn't go to school  because there were no ramps it's all  stays my father was scared I would fall  and hurt myself even now most schools  don't have ramps so many disabled  children can't go to school  she's always dreamed of getting an  education now she visits this High  School where teachers offer her tutoring  but it's not easy the challenge  Organization for Women with disabilities  aims to create a safe and friendly  environment for its members  it's a small but welcoming place for  more than 57 women who are disabled  here they meet and make handmade items  to sell like dolls pillows and blankets  sales help fund field trips and  activities  created the organization  women with disabilities in Libya suffer  from so many problems starting from  their homes many families do not build  ramps and only have stairs the bathrooms  are not accessible by wheelchairs so she  has to crawl this causes a lot of  frustration and psychological distress  we've met some girls who tried to commit  suicide  this parking lot in the heart of misrata  is close to Banks the city council a  courthouse and other public buildings  volunteers and organizations have marked  out a few parking spots for disabled  people  but without government backing there's  no control over who parks there and no  ramps to help the disabled get to them  Ramadan matig heads the Civil Society  Commission in misrata he says things are  slowly getting better  regime disabled people were completely  marginalized but after the revolution  many were wounded in the fighting and  thus created a shift in our society  looks at disabled people in general the  government is now researching on how to  renovate old buildings to make them  accessible for the disabled  although there aren't accurate numbers  the government estimates there are at  least 120  000 disabled people in Libya the actual  figure is probably far higher because of  war and conflict  in Libya women are widely believed to  suffer the most from the lack of  infrastructure but staff in  organizations like this say they help  ease the pain Malik trainer Al Jazeera  misrata
 

